-        "analysis": "'Do the Opposite: The Wealth Trick Rich People Use (Inversion Thinking)' is a three-part title: an imperative paradox ('Do the Opposite'), a secret-of-the-rich promise ('The Wealth Trick Rich People Use') and a named mental model in parentheses ('Inversion Thinking'). It contains no number, dollar figure, object or time marker, and 'the opposite' of what is never stated, so the curiosity gap is wide but vague. It belongs to the channel's 'named rule or mental model' family, which mostly lands in the TYPICAL middle: EwbXdhQ0qrw 'The 80/20 Rule of Money: Build Wealth by Doing Less' 0.90095x TYPICAL (published the day before), MV3oBl8OPfM 'Eat the Frog: The Wealth Rule Nobody Follows on Payday' 1.0592x TYPICAL. The one STRONG named-rule example, v-1VwbsUa94 'The Chinese Rule That Builds Wealth' 2.57622x, has a shorter, more concrete exotic hook. The 'what the rich actually do' framing performs best when it names a concrete mechanism: 7W0uvrGuOW4 'Buy, Borrow, Die: How the Rich Actually Avoid Taxes' 5.76468x STRONG, published the next day. Counterexamples on the same 'avoid mistakes' theme show the avoidance framing alone does not lift results: e06r3ZWyBVc 'Top 5 Wealth Killers You Have to Avoid' 0.82458x TYPICAL, NAg-Xx_xy8U '7 Hidden Ways Society Programs You to Stay Poor' 0.4978x WEAK, JtMc-fNjfpo 'Wealth Is Way Easier Than You Think' 0.49581x WEAK, and the investor-name title poD3PDHgAiE 'What Warren Buffett Would Do With $10,000 Today' 0.44562x WEAK. Within this sample, abstract mindset titles cluster at WEAK to low TYPICAL and concrete-mechanism titles at STRONG; this is descriptive across eight titles, not a proven cause. Note the title never mentions Munger or Buffett, although the script leans on them.",
