-        "analysis": "Full 21,036 canonical characters read in three contiguous chunks (0-10,000, 10,000-20,000, 20,000-21,036). The hook is a contrarian observation ('the smartest investors in the world seem to make money by doing the exact opposite') anchored by two famous names (Munger and Buffett) and a reframe ('They didn't spend their time figuring out how to get rich ... how to not stay poor'). Nick introduces himself within about 80 words, names the model ('inversion thinking'), and asks for a subscribe and like at about 1,000 characters, inside the hook window. The setup runs long: from about 1,264 to 8,549 (roughly a third of the video) the script argues for inversion through a stack of supporting devices (avoiding losses versus earning gains, Jacobi's 'invert, always invert', Munger's 'avoiding stupidity', the leaky-bucket metaphor, hunter-gatherer threat detection, lottery winners, Bezos's regret minimization, the 'every broke person has an excuse' observation) before the first list item appears. The bucket metaphor ('fix the holes first') is the clearest image in the video and earns its place; the Bezos detour weakens the frame because regret minimization asks what you would regret not doing, which is closer to the opposite of mistake avoidance, and the script then pivots it into regrets about car payments. The list itself is announced only at 'Let me show you exactly what I mean. The first way to guarantee you'll never build wealth', so the viewer does not know until a third of the way through that 11 items are coming, and the count is never given in advance. Transitions are ordinal and parallel ('The second guaranteed way to stay poor', 'The third way to guarantee permanent financial mediocrity', 'The fourth guaranteed method'), which keeps the structure legible but becomes repetitive; unlike many channel scripts there is no escalation ladder, so items feel equal weight. Only two items carry numbers (item 4, the $500 car payment opportunity cost; item 7, the $200 a month early-versus-late investor example) and these are the most concrete passages. Items 1, 7 and 9 have the best writing technique: item 1 uses the escalating salary ladder ($40,000 thinks 60,000, 60,000 thinks 80,000, 80,000 thinks six figures) and the teacher-versus-doctor contrast; item 9 uses four parallel 'You're stressed ... You're excited ... You're angry ... You're optimistic' sentences, then a commute analogy. Item 11 (home as a piggy bank) is a single short paragraph that names the behavior but never explains its cost or the opposite action before the script jumps to 'So, there you have it'; this reads as truncated. The close restates the frame ('11 ways to avoid financial disaster', 'stop being stupid with money') and returns to Munger, giving a neat bookend, but the only call to action is 'Start there' with no concrete first step. The inversion promise ('do the exact opposite') is never operationalised as a checklist of opposite actions. All stored text read; not an original-audio, editing or retention verification.",
+        "stored_text_completeness": "FULL_AVAILABLE_STORED_TEXT_READ_HASH_VERIFIED; auto-generated captions; original audio, timing and editing unverified",
+        "opening_words": "Have you ever noticed that the smartest investors in the world seem to make money by doing the exact opposite of what everyone else is doing? While most people are chasing the latest get-rich-quick scheme or trying to time the perfect market entry, billionaires like",
