         "claim_audit": [
-          "'The concept of inversion thinking originated with a German mathematician named Carl Jacobi ... His motto was simple: invert, always invert': Carl Gustav Jacob Jacobi was a 19th-century German mathematician, so the identity is correct. The maxim is usually given as 'man muss immer umkehren' and its popular English form comes from Munger ('the great algebraist Carl Jacobi ... Invert, always invert', as quoted in Schroeder's The Snowball, seen only via secondary excerpt). No dated primary Jacobi source was located in this review, and its original context was algebraic (reformulating problems, for example working with inverse functions), not a general 'consider what you're trying to avoid' heuristic. 'Originated with Jacobi' overstates it: reasoning backwards and proof by contradiction predate Jacobi. Attribution UNVERIFIED at primary level; the life-heuristic gloss is Munger's adaptation, not Jacobi's.",
-          "'Munger famously said that much of his success came not from being incredibly smart, but from consistently avoiding stupidity': a fair paraphrase of 'It is remarkable how much long-term advantage people like us have gotten by trying to be consistently not stupid, instead of trying to be very intelligent', which Quote Investigator traces to Munger's letter dated March 5, 1990 in Wesco Financial's 1989 annual report (available long before the 2025-12-12 publication). Supported as a paraphrase; the narrator does not quote it verbatim. The opening line that Munger and Buffett 'obsessed over ... how to not stay poor' is narrator framing, not a quote.",
-          "'Most lottery winners end up broke within a few years': overstated. Hankins, Hoekstra and Skiba (2011, Review of Economics and Statistics; about 35,000 Florida Fantasy 5 winners, prizes $600 to $150,000) found large prizes postponed rather than prevented bankruptcy: large winners were about 50% less likely to file immediately but more likely to file three to five years later, and roughly 2,000 of 35,000 winners were linked to a bankruptcy within five years. That supports 'winnings often do not fix underlying finances' but not 'most ... end up broke', and the study covers modest prizes, not 'millions of dollars'.",
-          "'20% annual interest will absolutely destroy your wealth': directionally supported and, if anything, conservative. Federal Reserve G.19 reported commercial-bank credit card rates on accounts assessed interest of about 21.91% (Q1 2025) and 22.25% (Q2 2025), available before publication (search excerpt; table column alignment flagged as ambiguous in the excerpt, so treat the exact values as approximate).",
-          "'Cash ... creates what psychologists call loss aversion': mislabelled. The effect described (paying cash feels more painful than swiping a card) is usually called the 'pain of paying' (Prelec and Loewenstein; Prelec and Simester's credit-card willingness-to-pay studies). Loss aversion (Kahneman and Tversky) is a different concept; the claim is a terminology error, and the specific studies were not re-checked in this review.",
-          "'That $500 car payment ... over 30 years that same money invested in index funds would grow to over $600,000': no rate is stated. Analyst calculation with monthly contributions and monthly compounding: $500 x 360 = $180,000 contributed; future value about $609,985 at 7%, $745,180 at 8%, $1,130,244 at 10%. The claim holds at 7% or above nominal, so it is plausible for historical nominal index-fund returns but is not inflation-adjusted and assumes no fees or taxes; a car payment usually does not run for 30 years, so the comparison assumes a perpetual car payment.",
-          "'A 25-year-old who invests just $200 a month for 10 years and then never invests another dime will actually end up with more money at retirement than someone who starts at 35 and invests the exact same amount for 30 years. The early investor puts in $24,000 total. The procrastinator puts in $72,000': contribution totals are correct ($200 x 120 = $24,000; $200 x 360 = $72,000). No rate or retirement age is stated; assuming both stop at 65 with monthly compounding, the early investor ends with about $400,130 versus $298,072 at 8% and $280,968 versus $243,994 at 7% (analyst calculation). The claim holds only above a break-even of about 6.1% annual; at lower returns the late investor wins, which the narrator does not disclose. 'Works three times as long' correctly reflects 30 versus 10 contribution years.",
-          "'Jeff Bezos ... leave his comfortable Wall Street job to start Amazon ... When I'm 80 years old ... regret minimization': consistent with Bezos's own widely reported account of leaving D. E. Shaw in 1994; not re-sourced in this review. Note this framework is about regretting inaction, not inversion of failure modes.",
-          "'Berkshire Hathaway ... has generated returns that have made thousands of shareholders wealthy simply by avoiding the mistakes that destroy most investors': the returns are well documented, but 'simply by avoiding mistakes' is a single-cause narrative; Berkshire's results also rest on insurance float, concentrated stock picks and acquisitions, which the narrator's own frame ('He didn't try to pick the perfect stocks') understates.",
-          "'There are teachers making $35,000 who retire as millionaires, and there are doctors making 300,000 who are completely broke', 'I've seen high earners ... more broke than minimum wage workers', 'the behaviors that keep people poor are remarkably consistent across all income brackets', 'this one shift ... has created more millionaires than any other mental framework': anecdotes and unsourced generalizations; UNVERIFIED. The income-is-not-the-problem framing ignores that a $35,000 income leaves much less margin than $300,000.",
-          "'When you stop doing things that drain your wealth, you'll notice the difference in your bank account within the first month' and 'building wealth becomes almost inevitable': unfalsifiable promises, not factual claims.",
-          "'Show me someone's five closest friends, and I can predict their financial future with scary accuracy': rhetorical claim, no study cited; UNVERIFIED.",
-          "'It's easier to avoid losing $20 than it is to earn $20': asserted as obvious, no evidence; plausible as a behavioral point but not tested.",
-          "Possible ASR or script artefacts preserved, not corrected against audio: 'which is exactly why it retailers love them', 'which honestly is a much lower than most people realize' (likely a missing word such as 'bar'), repeated 'They They' and 'And they They', sentence-initial 'Like', and filler tokens 'Um', 'uh'."
+          "SUPPORTED: Charlie Munger's 2007 USC address explicitly says problems often become easier when turned around, illustrates asking what does the worst damage and how to avoid it, and recommends asking what will fail. This supports the core inversion method. It does not establish that inversion itself created more millionaires than any other framework, that rich people generally use it, or that avoiding listed mistakes makes wealth inevitable.",
+          "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED: The script attributes 'invert, always invert' and the origin of the method to mathematician Carl Jacobi. Munger repeatedly popularized inversion and the Jacobi maxim is widely attributed, but the reviewed Munger speech transcript establishes inversion's use rather than a primary Jacobi document. The origin/wording remains secondary-source provenance in this audit.",
+          "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED: Berkshire's 2024 annual letter reports a 19.9% compounded annual gain in per-share market value for 1965-2024 versus 10.4% for the S&P 500 with dividends. That supports exceptional historical shareholder returns. It does not isolate 'simply avoiding mistakes' as the cause, and the script's 'thousands of shareholders wealthy' is not quantified.",
+          "UNVERIFIED: 'This one shift in perspective has created more millionaires than any other mental framework' is a comparative causal claim with no population, outcome definition or source in the stored text. 'Building wealth becomes almost inevitable' is likewise too strong; income, expenses, shocks, taxes, investment choice and returns still matter.",
+          "UNVERIFIED: 'Most lottery winners end up broke within a few years' is presented without a named dataset or study. No lottery population, jurisdiction, time horizon or definition of broke is supplied, so it is not used as evidence for inversion.",
+          "MISLEADING: 'The difference has nothing to do with how much money comes in' overstates the teacher/doctor anecdote. Spending, debt and behavior matter, but income directly affects feasible saving capacity. The examples are unsourced and cannot establish income irrelevance. The adjacent claim that every broke person has an excuse also collapses job loss, health, divorce and macro shocks into personal failure.",
+          "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED: The script's '20% annual interest' credit-card warning is realistic. The Federal Reserve's November 2025 report states average rates around 21.5% across all accounts and about 22.8% for accounts assessed interest at end-2024. But people who pay within the grace period do not incur finance charges, so card use and revolving high-interest debt should be distinguished.",
+          "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED: Prelec and Simester found higher willingness-to-pay in a credit-card condition for uncertain-value auction goods, but the effect was not always present; the second study's overall payment-method main effect was not significant and context mattered. The script's mechanistic statement that swiping removes pain and physical cash creates loss aversion is broader and more certain than this evidence.",
+          "SUPPORTED_WITH_ASSUMPTIONS: Investing $500 at each month-end for 30 years grows to about $609,986 at a nominal 7% annual rate compounded monthly, so 'over $600,000' reproduces near 7%. It is $180,000 with no return and about $745,180 at 8%. The script omits return, contribution timing, taxes, fees, inflation, volatility and interruption; it is a scenario, not the cost of every $500 car payment.",
+          "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED: IRS guidance confirms early retirement-plan or IRA withdrawals can be taxable and generally can face an additional 10% tax before age 59.5, subject to material exceptions; hardship distributions permanently reduce the plan balance. The script says one 'might' pay penalties and taxes, which is directionally sound, but account type, age and exception determine treatment.",
+          "SUPPORTED_WITH_ASSUMPTIONS: A person contributing $200 at each month-end from age 25 through 34 and then stopping can have more at 65 than someone contributing $200 monthly from 35 through 64, but only above roughly a 6.11% nominal annual return compounded monthly. At 7%, the modeled endpoints are about $280,968 versus $243,994; at 4%, the later saver has more ($138,810 versus $97,582). The script gives no return, fees, taxes, inflation or deposit-timing assumption, so presenting the outcome as automatic is misleading.",
+          "UNVERIFIED: 'Show me someone's five closest friends, and I can predict their financial future with scary accuracy' is not accompanied by a predictive study, measurement rule or accuracy rate. Munger's discussion of social-proof tendency supports the general possibility that peer behavior influences choices, not this quantitative-sounding prediction.",
+          "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED: The last item warns against repeatedly extracting home equity for consumption. That identifies leverage and housing-security risks, but the stored captions provide no rates, terms, loan-to-value, tax treatment or distinction between consumption, consolidation and productive uses. The abrupt transition prevents a full mechanism explanation."
         ]
